      "severity": "minor",
      "status": "resolved",
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**Migrated scenario code escaped the preserved Clippy and native-check gates.**\n\n- **Expected:** The preserved `quality / clippy` job (`cargo +1.92.0 clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`) and the `quality / native` matrix keep linting and compiling the service-dependent scenario code. Before this sprint that code lived in `src/tests/tests_tokio.rs` and `src/tests/tests_msgpack.rs`, which `--all-targets` covered as lib test code.\n- **Observed:** The scenarios moved to `[[test]] name = \"e2e\" ... test = false`. Cargo's `--all-targets` expands to `--tests`, and `--tests` selects only targets with `test = true`. As a result, `tests/e2e/**` is never linted by Clippy and never compiled by the pinned/stable `quality / native` matrix. I confirmed this with `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features --message-format=json`: it emits no `e2e` artifact. The `e2e / *` jobs compile the target with `-D warnings` but do not run Clippy. Explicit `cargo clippy --test e2e --all-features -- -D warnings` is clean today, so this is a gate-coverage regression rather than a current lint.\n- **Repair:** Add the target explicitly in `quality.yml`, e.g. `cargo +1.92.0 clippy --all-targets --test e2e --all-features -- -D warnings` and `cargo +${{ matrix.toolchain }} check --all-targets --test e2e --all-features`. Extend `WorkflowContractTests.QualityWorkflowPreservesTheQualityMatrices` to require `--test e2e` in both commands so the coverage cannot silently drop again.",
      "regressionRefs": [
        {
          "repositoryId": "rayx-signalr",
          "path": "dotnet/RayX.SignalR.E2E.Tests/Contracts/WorkflowContractTests.cs"
        },
        {
          "repositoryId": "rayx-signalr",
          "path": ".github/workflows/quality.yml"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S1-F2",
      "boundaryId": "S1",
      "criterionIds": [
        "R1.C2"
      ],
      "severity": "minor",
      "status": "resolved",
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**Cells that fail before their scenario process starts write no `artifacts-temp/e2e/<cell>.json` and lose the Aspire resource logs.**\n\n- **Expected (R1.C2):** On cell failure, bounded and redacted output plus Aspire resource logs are written to `artifacts-temp/e2e/<cell>.json`. This is the failure-only artifact CI uploads.\n- **Observed:** `E2ECellTests.Cell` writes diagnostics only when a runner returns `!outcome.Passed`. Two failure paths throw before that point:\n  - `AspireEnvironment.EnsureStartedAsync` fails, for example when a completion-gated resource exits non-zero (provisioning, fixture build or bindgen) or the service or `fixture-host` never becomes healthy.\n  - `NativeE2EBinary.BuildAsync` fails (`cargo test --test e2e --no-run`).\n\n  Neither path writes a file, and the catch in `EnsureStartedAsync` calls `StopAsync()`, which disposes the app before any resource logs are collected. The uploaded `artifacts-temp/e2e` is then empty for exactly the infrastructure failures most likely on the first ubuntu/windows/macos CI runs. A resource that never becomes healthy is also only detected by the 20-minute startup timeout, and every later cell repeats it. With a 45-minute job limit, the job is killed before it can report anything useful.\n- **Repair:**\n  - Capture `AllResourceLogsAsync()` (redacted, bounded) before `StopAsync()` in the startup failure path.\n  - Catch startup and native-build exceptions in `Cell`, write `CellDiagnostics` for the cell (exception message plus the captured resource logs and build stderr tail), then fail.\n  - Make the health waits fail fast when the resource reaches a terminal state, instead of waiting out the startup timeout.\n  - Cover this with a focused `Catalog` or `Orchestration` test that forces a startup failure, or at least a unit test of the diagnostics-on-exception path.",
      "regressionRefs": [
